### What are the main concurrency tools in Java?

Java's concurrency toolkit has grown significantly since Java 1.0. The tools form layers, from low-level primitives to high-level abstractions.

#### Low-level primitives

- `Thread` and `Runnable` for basic execution.
- `synchronized` for mutual exclusion.
- `volatile` for memory visibility.
- `wait()`, `notify()`, `notifyAll()` for thread signaling.

#### Mid-level (java.util.concurrent)

- `ExecutorService` for thread pool management.
- `Future` for async results.
- `ReentrantLock` for flexible locking with `tryLock()` and `lockInterruptibly()`.
- `Condition` for targeted thread signaling.

#### High-level synchronizers

- `CountDownLatch` for waiting on N events.
- `CyclicBarrier` for N threads synchronizing at a checkpoint.
- `Semaphore` for limiting concurrent access.
- `Phaser` for flexible multi-phase synchronization.

#### Concurrent collections

- `ConcurrentHashMap` for thread-safe maps with lock striping.
- `CopyOnWriteArrayList` for read-heavy lists.
- `BlockingQueue` for producer-consumer patterns.

#### Atomics

- `AtomicInteger`, `AtomicLong` for lock-free counters.
- `LongAdder`, `LongAccumulator` for high-contention accumulators.

#### Modern Java

- `CompletableFuture` (Java 8) for async pipelines.
- Virtual threads (Java 21) for lightweight concurrency.

### How does CountDownLatch work?

CountDownLatch(N) starts with count N. Any thread can call `countDown()` to decrement. Threads calling `await()` block until the count reaches zero. It is one-time: once triggered, it stays at zero.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class LatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Accounting"); latch.countDown(); });
        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Loans");      latch.countDown(); });
        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Compliance"); latch.countDown(); });

        latch.await(); // blocks until count = 0
        System.out.println("All reports ready. Generating EOD summary.");

        pool.shutdown();
    }

    static void generateReport(String dept) {
        System.out.println(dept + " report generated");
    }
}
```

```text
Accounting report generated
Loans report generated
Compliance report generated
All reports ready. Generating EOD summary.
```

### How does CyclicBarrier work?

CyclicBarrier(N) blocks each of N threads until all N have called `await()`. When the Nth thread arrives, all are released simultaneously. It auto-resets for the next trip.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BarrierDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(3, () -> {
            System.out.println("--- All 3 reached checkpoint ---");
        });
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    System.out.println("Auditor-" + id + " phase-1 done");
                    barrier.await(); // wait for others
                    System.out.println("Auditor-" + id + " phase-2 done");
                    barrier.await(); // reusable! wait again
                    System.out.println("Auditor-" + id + " complete");
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            });
        }
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Auditor-0 phase-1 done
Auditor-1 phase-1 done
Auditor-2 phase-1 done
--- All 3 reached checkpoint ---
Auditor-0 phase-2 done
Auditor-1 phase-2 done
Auditor-2 phase-2 done
--- All 3 reached checkpoint ---
Auditor-0 complete
Auditor-1 complete
Auditor-2 complete
```

### How does Semaphore work?

Semaphore limits concurrent access to a resource by issuing a fixed number of permits.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SemaphoreDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Semaphore semaphore = new Semaphore(3); // max 3 concurrent
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    semaphore.acquire();
                    System.out.println("Thread-" + id + " accessing database");
                    Thread.sleep(200); // simulate DB work
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                } finally {
                    semaphore.release();
                }
            });
        }
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### How is CountDownLatch different from CyclicBarrier?

| Feature | CountDownLatch | CyclicBarrier |
|---|---|---|
| Reusable | No (one-time) | Yes (auto-resets) |
| Who counts/waits | Different threads can count and wait | Same threads that wait are counted |
| Reset | Cannot reset | Automatic after each trip |
| Barrier action | None | Optional Runnable on trip |
| Use case | Wait for N events | N threads sync at a point |
| Broken state | N/A | BrokenBarrierException |

### What is the concurrency toolkit summary?

| Tool | Purpose | Banking Example |
|---|---|---|
| Thread/Runnable | Basic execution | One teller per customer |
| ExecutorService | Pool management | 50-teller counter |
| synchronized | Mutual exclusion | Vault access |
| volatile | Visibility | Shutdown flag |
| ReentrantLock | Flexible locking | Account transfer lock |
| AtomicInteger | Lock-free counter | Transaction counter |
| ConcurrentHashMap | Thread-safe map | Session cache |
| BlockingQueue | Producer-consumer | Trade settlement queue |
| CountDownLatch | Wait for N events | EOD report aggregation |
| CyclicBarrier | Phase synchronization | Multi-phase audit |
| Semaphore | Limit concurrency | DB connection limit |
| CompletableFuture | Async pipelines | Loan approval workflow |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- When would you use a Lock instead of synchronized? When you need tryLock(), lockInterruptibly(), or multiple conditions.
- What is the difference between fair and non-fair Semaphore? Fair respects FIFO order but has higher overhead.
- Can you use CountDownLatch.await() with a timeout? Yes, `await(timeout, unit)` returns false on timeout.
